/**
\page usage Usage Examples

Practical, copy-pasteable recipes for the five samplers. Every snippet uses
`Real = double`; substitute `float` or your project's `Real` as needed. All
generators follow the same lifecycle: **construct → `define(...)` → call**.

\tableofcontents

\section usage_build Building and running the demo

The bundled driver runs the test suite and writes 100k samples from each
generator to `samples_*.txt`:

\code{.sh}
cd cpp
make
./main.exe
\endcode

\section usage_bikappa Bi-kappa velocity samples

Sample a 3D bi-kappa velocity distribution, anisotropic about the field
direction `ub`, and stream the samples to a file.

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_kappa_distribution.H"
#include <fstream>
using Real = double;

bi_kappa_distribution<Real> biKappa;
//             kappa  theta_perp  theta_par  ub          cap                    seed
biKappa.define(2.0,   1.0,        2.0,       {0, 0, 1},  Real(INFINITY),        20030410);

std::ofstream out("samples_bikappa.txt");
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; ++i) {
    bi_kappa_distribution<Real>::point_type v = biKappa();   // {vx, vy, vz}, global frame
    out << v[0] << ' ' << v[1] << ' ' << v[2] << '\n';
}
\endcode

Write the cap as `bi_kappa_distribution<Real>::no_cap()` rather than `INFINITY` in real
code; it is spelled out above only to keep the column alignment readable. **The cap is off
by default**, so `biKappa.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1})` samples the same law — the cap is
named here only because the seed has to follow it as the sixth argument.

\warning The cap selects which of **two different distributions** you sample. Omitting it, or
passing `no_cap()`, gives the bi-kappa distribution. Passing a finite value gives the bi-kappa
distribution *conditioned on* a component-wise box — a bounded law with its own normalization,
whose tails and moments differ, and which is **not axisymmetric about** `ub` because the box is
a cube in normalized components. For `kappa <= 3/2` no practical cap value is a good
approximation to the unconditioned law. Use `dist.param().capped()` to check which is active.

\subsection usage_bikappa_cap Selecting the target law: uncapped or capped

**Uncapped is the default, so omitting the argument is enough.** These three are identical:

\code{.cpp}
using BK = bi_kappa_distribution<Real>;
BK dist;

dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1});                                  // omit it
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, BK::no_cap());                    // name it
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, std::numeric_limits<Real>::infinity());
\endcode

`no_cap()` *is* `+infinity`; naming it is worth the keystrokes wherever a reader of the calling
code needs to know which of the two laws is being sampled.

Uncapped is not merely a cap large enough to never bind: `param_type::capped()` is
`std::isfinite(...)`, so the sampler skips the acceptance-rejection loop entirely and the core
mapping runs exactly once per draw. No rejection, no retry limit, no discarded variates.

To sample the **capped** law instead, pass a finite positive value. The arguments are
positional, so enabling a cap means passing `ub` as well:

\code{.cpp}
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1}, 50.0);   // bi-kappa conditioned on a 50-theta box
bool c = dist.param().capped();                // true
\endcode

To go back to the unconditioned law afterwards, assign the sentinel — **do not pass `0`**, which
`param_type` rejects along with every other non-positive value:

\code{.cpp}
dist.max_normalized_velocity(BK::no_cap());    // capped -> uncapped
dist.max_normalized_velocity(0.0);             // throws std::invalid_argument
\endcode

\section usage_bimaxwell Bi-Maxwellian velocity samples

Same pattern, without the `kappa` parameter.

\code{.cpp}
#include "bi_maxwellian_distribution.H"
using Real = double;

bi_maxwellian_distribution<Real> biMaxwell;
//               theta_perp  theta_par  ub          cap    seed
biMaxwell.define(1.0,        2.0,       {0, 0, 1},  20.0,  20030410);

bi_maxwellian_distribution<Real>::point_type v = biMaxwell();
\endcode

\section usage_reproducible Reproducible runs (seeding)

Each sampler owns an internal `std::mt19937`. Seed it for byte-for-byte
reproducible sequences; re-seeding with the same value restarts the stream.

\code{.cpp}
bi_kappa_distribution<Real> dist;

// (a) seed as the last define() argument (bi_kappa only).  The cap cannot be
//     omitted here, because the seed is the argument after it:
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0, {0, 0, 1},
            bi_kappa_distribution<Real>::no_cap(), /*seed*/ 55555);

// (b) or seed any time after define() (all samplers):
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 2.0);
dist.seed(55555);            // same seed  -> identical sequence
dist.seed(-1);               // negative   -> unpredictable seed from std::random_device
\endcode

\section usage_external_rng Sharing one RNG across samplers

Pass your own `std::mt19937` to `operator()` when several samplers should draw
from a single stream (e.g. for coordinated reproducibility).

\code{.cpp}
#include <random>
std::mt19937 gen(42u);

bi_kappa_distribution<Real> perp, par;
perp.define(2.0, 1.0, 1.0);
par.define (2.0, 1.0, 5.0);

auto vp = perp(gen);   // both consume the same gen
auto vq = par (gen);
\endcode

\section usage_redefine Reconfiguring a sampler

Reconfigure everything with `define(...)`, or change one parameter at a time
with the convenience setters — each setter re-validates the full parameter set.

\code{.cpp}
bi_kappa_distribution<Real> dist;
dist.define(2.0, 1.0, 1.0);     // initial configuration

dist.kappa(5.0);                // change one field...
dist.theta_par(2.5);            // ...each rebuilds + re-validates
dist.ub({1, 2, 3});

dist.define(3.0, 1.5, 2.5, {0, 0, 1});         // or replace the whole config at once
\endcode

\note `max_normalized_velocity(...)` is the one setter that changes *which distribution* is
sampled, not merely its parameters.

\section usage_velocity Custom speed-squared distribution

`general_velocity_generator` draws a speed from any speed-squared PDF `g(w)`
you supply (by rejection sampling, with `w = |V|²`) and assigns an isotropic 3D
direction, so `|V| = sqrt(w)`. There is no mass parameter: for a distribution
originally posed in energy, `w = |V|² = 2E/m`, so the mass drops out.

\code{.cpp}
#include "general_velocity_generator.H"
#include <cmath>
using Real = double;

auto gW = [](Real w) -> Real { return std::exp(-0.5 * w); };   // any positive g(w), w = |v|^2

general_velocity_generator<Real> velGen;
//            g    v2_min  v2_max
velGen.define(gW,  0.0,    40.0);
velGen.seed(20030410);

general_velocity_generator<Real>::point_type v = velGen();
\endcode

\section usage_field_aligned Field-aligned velocity (anisotropic)

`field_aligned_velocity_generator` builds an anisotropic velocity about the
field direction `ub`: the parallel component draws `v_par² ` from your
speed-squared PDF `g(w)` (so `|v_par| = sqrt(w)`), pointing along `ub` for
`sign = +1` or opposite it for `sign = -1`, while the two perpendicular
components are a Maxwellian with thermal speed `theta_perp` (each
`~ N(0, theta_perp²/2)`, the same convention as `bi_maxwellian_distribution`).
The result is returned in the global frame.

\code{.cpp}
#include "field_aligned_velocity_generator.H"
#include <cmath>
using Real = double;

auto gW = [](Real w) -> Real { return std::exp(-0.5 * w); };   // any positive g(w), w = v_par^2

field_aligned_velocity_generator<Real> faGen;
//           g    v2_min  v2_max  theta_perp  ub          sign
faGen.define(gW,  0.0,    40.0,   1.0,        {0, 0, 1},  +1);
faGen.seed(20030410);

field_aligned_velocity_generator<Real>::point_type v = faGen();   // {vx, vy, vz}, global frame
\endcode

\section usage_position Custom spatial density

`general_position_generator` draws positions in 1D/2D/3D from any density
`rho(x)` by rejection sampling in an axis-aligned box. The returned point is a
`std::vector<Real>` of length `dimension`.

\code{.cpp}
#include "general_position_generator.H"
#include <cmath>
using Real = double;
using pos_t = general_position_generator<Real>::point_type;   // std::vector<Real>

auto rho = [](const pos_t& x) -> Real { return 1.0 + std::sin(x[0]) * std::sin(x[1]); };

general_position_generator<Real> posGen;
const Real pi = 3.14159265358979;
//            dim  lower         upper        density
posGen.define(2,   {-pi, -pi},   {pi, pi},    rho);
posGen.seed(20030410);

pos_t x = posGen();   // length-2 vector
\endcode

\section usage_errors Error handling

- The general and field-aligned velocity generators throw `std::runtime_error`
  if called before `define(...)`.
- `define(...)` throws `std::invalid_argument` on bad input (e.g. `kappa <= 0.5`,
  non-positive `theta`/`cap`, `v2_min >= v2_max`, `v2_min < 0`, a zero `ub`,
  `sign` other than ±1, `dimension` outside 1–3).
- Velocity samplers throw `std::runtime_error` if the cap rejection loop fails
  10⁶ times in a row.

\code{.cpp}
try {
    general_velocity_generator<Real> g;
    auto v = g();                 // not define()d yet -> throws
} catch (const std::runtime_error& e) {
    // handle "must be define(...)d before sampling"
}
\endcode

\section usage_full Complete worked example

The full driver below configures all four generators and writes their samples.
See `test_suite.H` for further patterns (reproducible seeding, cap enforcement,
and redefinition).

\include main.cpp
*/
